      "publication_year": 2012,
      "publication_date": "2012-09-01",
      "abstract": "The work of Slavoj &#381;i&#382;ek has long been known for its idiosyncratic superimposition of Lacanian, Hegelian, and Marxian themes. While &#381;i&#382;ek&#8217;s re-elaboration of Lacan, and partly of Hegel, has attracted an enormous amount of critical attention, his appropriation of Marx remains relatively unexplored. In this paper, I intend to carry out a systematic analysis of the role played by Marx in relation to Hegel in two of &#381;i&#382;ek recent writings. Initially, the authors in question appear to be, against all expectations, repeatedly opposed to each other, with conflicting results. On the one hand, Hegel&#8217;s philosophy of religion would be the only philosophy which allows us appropriately to approach the question concerning human nature and the related problem of freedom in a materialist way, while Marx&#8217;s critique of religion would propose a &#8216;direct dis-alienation&#8217; that would ultimately rely on an idealist bias. On the other hand, &#381;i&#382;ek&#8217;s own &#8216;communist hypothesis&#8217; tacitly recuperates the early Marx&#8217;s notion of critique and, precisely in doing so, unveils a version of Hegelianism that is irreparably conservative.",
